<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vojvodina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vojvodina is an autonomous province of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, covering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a>, the Serbian part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Srem</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novi-sad">Novi Sad</a>. Long part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands, it took its name from the Serbian Vojvodina proclaimed in 1848. It joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> in 1918 and remains ethnically mixed, with a large Hungarian minority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vojvodina.jpg" alt="Vojvodina" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Vojvodina · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Vojvodina.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> the Starčevo and Vinča cultures flourished in the area. Under Roman rule, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> (modern Sremska Mitrovica) was one of the four capital cities of the empire, and six emperors were born in or near it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a> belonged to the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a> lay outside the empire and was held by the Sarmatian Iazyges. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> ruled the area, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settled there in the 6th and 7th centuries. The region belonged to the Principality and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> from the 10th century until the 16th.</p><p>After the Hungarian defeat at Mohács in 1526, the Serb mercenary leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovan-nenad">Jovan Nenad</a> created a short-lived state with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subotica">Subotica</a> as its capital and proclaimed himself Serbian Emperor; Hungarian nobles defeated his troops in 1527 and he was assassinated. Ottoman rule then lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> of 1699 and the Austrian conquest of 1716, confirmed at Passarowitz in 1718. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> who settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands received rights in exchange for serving in the Military Frontier, and Germans were settled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> to repopulate the land.</p><p>At the May Assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Sremski Karlovci</a> in 1848 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> declared the Serbian Vojvodina, an autonomous region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, and war between Serbs and Hungarians followed, with atrocities against civilians on both sides. In 1849 the emperor made it the crown land of the Voivodeship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Temeschwar</a>, which lasted until 1860. After 1867 it returned to Hungarian administration. The 1910 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 34% Serbs, 28% Hungarians and 21% Germans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 25 November 1918 an assembly of 757 deputies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novi-sad">Novi Sad</a> proclaimed the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/backa">Bačka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baranja">Baranja</a> with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and on 1 December 1918 the region became part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>. From 1929 to 1941 it formed part of the Danube Banovina. In the Second World War Hungary annexed Bačka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baranya">Baranya</a> and the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a>. Axis authorities killed about 50,000 people there, mostly Serbs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, and the Jewish population was almost completely killed or deported. Vojvodina is today an autonomous province of Serbia, with about 1.7 million inhabitants and Novi Sad as its administrative centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vojvodina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Serbia united with <b>Vojvodina</b>, and on 28 November 1918 it absorbed the Kingdom of Montenegro at the Podgorica Assembly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom united some twelve million people in 1921, rising to about sixteen million by 1941, from regions with very different pasts: former Habsburg Slovenia, Croatia, <b>Vojvodina</b> and Bosnia-Herzegovina, and the formerly Ottoman lands of Serbia, Montenegro, Kosovo and Macedonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The six republics, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Croatia, Macedonia, Montenegro, Serbia and Slovenia, were constitutionally equal; Serbia alone contained two autonomous provinces, Kosovo and <b>Vojvodina</b>, which the 1974 constitution gave voting power equal to the republics', to the lasting anger of Serbian officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Serbian tradition remembers him as a forerunner of later Serbian autonomy in <b>Vojvodina</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/realm-of-jovan-nenad">Realm of Jovan Nenad</a></li><li><blockquote>A national assembly at Sremski Karlovci in May 1848 proclaimed the Serbian <b>Vojvodina</b> in Srem, Baranya, Bačka and Banat, elected Josif Rajačić patriarch and Colonel Stevan Šupljikac voivode, and set up a Main Board led by Đorđe Stratimirović.</blockquote><a href="/state/serbian-vojvodina">Serbian Vojvodina</a></li><li><blockquote>On 29 October the Croatian Sabor broke ties with Hungary and Austria, and the new state was proclaimed, covering Slovenia, Croatia-Slavonia, Dalmatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina and <b>Vojvodina</b>, with the Slovene politician Anton Korošec as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a></li></ul></section>
